let's get back to Brighton and the  Labour Party conference because we're  joined now by the former foreign  secretary and deputy Labour leader Dame  Margaret Beckett thank you very much for  being with us  now I'm keen to talk to you about brexit  but before we get there I just want to  find out about some of the movement  shall we say internal wranglings that  the Labour Party has been going through  the last couple of days because you're  on the National Executive Committee when  you're at the meeting when Jon's  Landsman filed this motion to get rid of  the post of deputy leader effectively  I'll sting Tom Watson no I was on the  m25 so you were stuck he's stuck for a  long time and what do you make of that  move I thought it was very unwise as I  think most people now agree because and  it's not an attack in any way it's  inevitable that you in the media are  talking about that and as John Ashworth  said very Abele earlier on that's not  what we all need to be talking about  because that's not what we're here for  you were stuck in them 25 and Tom Watson  herself couldn't even be at the meeting  because of childcare reasons oh it  sounds a bit like a cave no I think it  was just one of those things it seemed  to John like a good idea so he suggested  it do you think it's quite a ball that  Jeremy Corbyn didn't know what was  happening if Jeremy was supposed to keep  abreast with every little thought or  idea and the John Landsman is thought of  he'd go crazy well let's move on to  practicality and Labour's current  position on breakfast is to try and win  a general election obviously and try and  renegotiate a brexit deal and then have  a second referendum which remain and  leave on the ballot paper but it hasn't  said crucially in which way it would  campaign is that acceptable well it's  what that's one of the things that we're  going to have to stretch out and there  isn't any doubt there'll be different  points of view I think it's entirely  understandable whichever side of the  discussion the argument you come down on  everybody's really worried about what's  happening in their constituencies what  could happen in their constituencies  depending on how things go and I'm  really sincere and wanting to get the  best way through and I think Jeremy's  been quite smart  actually in concentrating on trying to  keep people to bring people together and  to keep people together there may come a  point when we have to make more of a  choice that's exactly the kind of thing  what's your view my view I'm afraid is  is very clear you like everybody else or  most people anyway  in 2016 I accepted that we were going to  leave the European Union I listen to all  the things that were said by the then  Prime Minister about how you should stay  close and you know have a good  constructive relationship and preserve  as much as we could of what was good  about our being in the European Union  and I swallowed all that and then  suddenly she steps forward and Lancaster  House and lays down all these totally  unrealistic crazy red lines that would  would likely to destroy many of the  advantages and benefits  we'd had from being in the European  Union and from then on for me really it  was downhill all the way because the  more it went on and the more to my mind  dangerous it got the more worried I was  about whether there was a credible deal  and you know I I spent many years 10 or  11 in the cabinet and most of them  negotiating in in the European Union or  on behalf of the European Union and the  idea that we would be in some way linked  to and bound to the rules and  regulations that the European Union step  sets on all sorts of good things workers  protection environment was on without  having any say of them without even  being in the room the ball I thought  about that law I panicked so I am now  the view personally that we of course  must ask the people whether this is  still what they had in mind and then in  in that conversation I would say we  should stay because none of the deals on  offer not one of them there's any  comparison to the deal we have now but  look if you just look at what's  happening with the Lib Dems and who were  polling within touching distance at  labor  are you not worried that by not taking a  more decisively remained position labor  are out of step with the members but  also a lot of your voters yes I am  worried about that and I think everybody  is nobody has the right answer here  there are difficulties on all sides  everybody's really worried about what's  the best way to stance to take what's  the best way to approach it and that's  what we're here to discuss and thrash  out at this conference so yes I can't  worry about that but I completely accept  the legitimacy of the argument by people  who say that's the best thing to do  Jeremy Corbyn won't even say if he would  vote leave or remain in a referendum is  that a problem oh you know if you're  trying to say as he is let's not close  the door for people and irrespective of  their point of view then you're bound to  end up saying we'll hang on a minute we  don't have to decide that yet but now  whether that would work whether it is  credible whether it's whether the  electorate would accept it that's  exactly the sort of thing we're trying  to thrash out this week now you were  first elected to Parliament in 1974 that  means you've seen Howard Wilson  Tony Blair led with two big election  winners in action in absorbs the Mori  poll out this week says that Jeremy  Corbyn has got the lowest approval  ratings of any opposition leader all the  way back to we're not Serb it began in  1977  what is going on  well Jeremy is an unusual leader he  comes through a very different  background in politics through most of  the previous leaders and he has heard I  think I know this is a complaint in you  this is the water we swim in but he has  had I think an unusually and continually  harsh treatment by some elements in the  news media and obviously that doesn't  help is the brexit position a lot of the  problem as well what some people see is  a lack of clarity it could be you know  some people like to be able to read what  they want into what people say others  want a clear now I want to know make  this or that it could be one of the  difficulties but I don't want to over  egg this because I think it would be a  big mistake  no general election is like the previous  general election there isn't any doubt  in 2017 when people saw Jeremy if you  like more unfettered that there was a  very different reaction to him and I  know many of the people around Jeremy  are hoping that would be the case again  I certainly hope so too but for me it's  a very clear simple equation there is  only one Prime Minister in Britain is it  going to be Boris Johnson or is it going  to Jeremy Corbyn and I know where Mike  Ross is going just finally I'm talking  about the next prime minister there has  been some talk about a government of  national unity if Boris Johnson resigns  or if he's forced out and around the  time of the extension or the deadline  for breakfast  I was reading a piece and spectator over  the last couple of days which suggested  that your name was being put forward to  as a potential leader of a government of  national unity I mean is that something  that you would consider if it was a way  to stop the New Deal brexit I haven't  seen this metadata piece and we I mean I  completely understand the my government  my people looking at all what's going on  start talking about a government of  national unity I have to admit I'm not a  great believer and it's likely both  myself so I haven't spent any time I'm  agonizing over whether I would but you  would always help  I never rule anything out in politics  but I think a government of national  unity is pretty unlikely and we do  actually have a candidate for Prime  Minister thank you very much our  demographic for being on the program

